# FIT-GGUF pins SHA-256 over the exact bytes of checked-in files: Fidelity
# Registry guard profiles, reference manifests, calibration records, evaluator
# contracts and the eval-v1 corpus slices. Git for Windows defaults to
# `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout and makes every
# one of those digests fail on an otherwise clean clone (`fit registry verify`
# reports `guard_profile sha256 mismatch`).
#
# Disable end-of-line translation for the whole repository so a checkout
# reproduces the committed bytes verbatim on every platform. Do not narrow this
# to specific paths: any text file whose bytes are hashed would be a silent
# integrity hole.
#
# Consequence to keep in mind: with translation off, a Windows `.cmd`/`.bat`
# script committed with LF stays LF on checkout, and cmd.exe mis-parses
# line continuations in LF-only batch files. Write such scripts with explicit
# CRLF and commit those bytes.
* -text
